Rules:
- Give the property's address only if the user's last message gives it or changes it, and
  null if it does not.
- Give an address, the property's or a place's, as the user wrote it, with every part they
  gave, such as the street, the house number, the postcode or the district, and add no part
  they did not give, except as the next two rules say.
- If the last message completes or corrects an address so far, such as with a house number,
  give the whole address: the address so far with that change.
- If the user picks one of the places that you offered for an address, give that place's
  address as you wrote it, without a district in brackets.
- A message can name other addresses, such as an estate agent's office or a workplace. Only
  the one to check is the property; a place that the user travels to regularly, such as their
  work, is one of their places; any other address, such as the estate agent's office, is
  neither.
- Give the places the user travels to only if the last message adds one, changes one or drops
  one, or says that the user travels nowhere. Then give the whole list as the message leaves
  it: each place so far that stays, with its ID, and each new place without one. Give an empty
  list if the user travels nowhere, and null if the message changes none of the places.
- For a place so far, give its ID and its name, and null for its address and for each time
  that the last message does not change: they stay as they were.
- A place may be outside Berlin. A place that the user names but does not describe yet, such
  as "I will send the second one later", gets a name only.
- A time is the clock time when the user leaves: home, for the place, or the place, for home.
  Give it as HH:MM on the 24-hour clock, such as 07:45 or 17:30. A vague time, such as "in the
  morning", is null, and so is a time when the user must arrive, such as "I have to be there by
  nine": never work out from it when they leave.
